/**
\defgroup distrib_core The structure of a distribution
\ingroup distrib
\brief Common structure, parameter descriptors, symmetric tensors, and interface shared by all univariate distributions.

Functions in this module are marked `LAPLACE_HD` and can be called on the host and inside Kokkos kernels on the device.

## Overview

In Laplace, a univariate distribution (a distrib) is a lightweight C++ structure whose non-static fields are its natural parameters \f$\theta\f$. A distribution is constructed per observation and copied by value into Kokkos kernels. Every statistical quantity is evaluated through a const member function, with separate functions for each derivative order up to order 6.

A distribution is either continuous or discrete, distinguished by the enumeration \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_kind "distrib_kind":
- `distrib_kind::continuous`: continuous density with respect to Lebesgue measure on \f$\mathbb{R}\f$. Provides `logpdf()`, `quantile()`, and continuous cumulative distribution functions.
- `distrib_kind::discrete`: probability mass function on integer observations. Provides `logpmf()` and discrete cumulative distribution functions.

## Member functions

Every distribution struct provides a uniform set of static members and evaluation functions:

| Member | Return type | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `kind` | `distrib_kind` | Distribution kind (`continuous` or `discrete`). |
| `n_params` | `int` | Number of natural parameters \f$P\f$. |
| `params` | `const distrib_param_info[P]` | Descriptors of each natural parameter. |
| `logpdf(y)` / `logpmf(y)` | `double` | Log-density \f$\log f(y; \theta)\f$ or log-mass \f$\log p(y; \theta)\f$. |
| `logpdf_d1(y)` ... `_d6(y)` | `sym_tensor<P, K>` | Partial derivatives \f$\frac{\partial^K}{\partial\theta^K} \log f(y; \theta)\f$ for order \f$K \in \{1, \dots, 6\}\f$. |
| `logpmf_d1(y)` ... `_d6(y)` | `sym_tensor<P, K>` | Partial derivatives \f$\frac{\partial^K}{\partial\theta^K} \log p(y; \theta)\f$ for order \f$K \in \{1, \dots, 6\}\f$. |
| `logpdf_dy_dk<J, K>(y)` | `sym_tensor<P, K>` | Mixed partial derivatives \f$\frac{\partial^{J+K}}{\partial y^J \partial\theta^K} \log f(y; \theta)\f$ with \f$J\f$ derivatives in \f$y\f$ and order-\f$K\f$ symmetric tensor in \f$\theta\f$ (\f$J + K \le 6\f$; continuous distributions only). |
| `expected_info()` | `sym_tensor<P, 2>` | Expected Fisher information matrix \f$I(\theta) = -\mathbb{E}\left[\frac{\partial^2}{\partial\theta^2} \log f(Y; \theta)\right]\f$. |
| `expected_info_d1()` ... `_d4()` | `expected_info_tensor<P, K>` | Partial derivatives \f$\frac{\partial^K}{\partial\theta^K} I_{ab}(\theta)\f$ for order \f$K \in \{1, \dots, 4\}\f$. |
| `cdf(y)` | `double` | Cumulative distribution function \f$F(y) = \mathbb{P}(Y \le y)\f$. |
| `ccdf(y)` | `double` | Complementary cumulative distribution function \f$1 - F(y) = \mathbb{P}(Y > y)\f$. |
| `log_cdf(y)` | `double` | Logarithm of the cumulative distribution function, \f$\log F(y)\f$. |
| `log_ccdf(y)` | `double` | Logarithm of the complementary cumulative distribution function, \f$\log(1 - F(y))\f$. |
| `log_cdf_d1(y)` ... `_d6(y)` | `sym_tensor<P, K>` | Partial derivatives \f$\frac{\partial^K}{\partial\theta^K} \log F(y; \theta)\f$ for order \f$K \in \{1, \dots, 6\}\f$. |
| `log_ccdf_d1(y)` ... `_d6(y)` | `sym_tensor<P, K>` | Partial derivatives \f$\frac{\partial^K}{\partial\theta^K} \log(1 - F(y; \theta))\f$ for order \f$K \in \{1, \dots, 6\}\f$. |
| `quantile(p)` | `double` | Quantile function \f$F^{-1}(p)\f$ for \f$p \in (0, 1)\f$ (continuous distributions only). |
| `support_lower()` | `double` | Lower limit of the support at \f$\theta\f$. |
| `support_upper()` | `double` | Upper limit of the support at \f$\theta\f$. |
| `center()` | `double` | A rough central location (for the pilot distributions \f$\mu\f$), a hint for placing quadrature nodes. |
| `scale()` | `double` | A rough spread (a standard deviation or a scale parameter), a hint for the width of quadrature panels. |
| `param_kinks(j, y)` | `distrib_kinks` | Points of parameter \f$\theta_j\f$ where \f$\log f\f$ fails to be smooth at observation \f$y\f$. |
| `y_regularity` | `int` | Differentiability order of \f$\log f\f$ in \f$y\f$ (\ref laplace::distrib::infinitely_smooth "infinitely_smooth" or non-negative integer; continuous distributions only). |
| `y_kinks()` | `distrib_kinks` | Points of \f$y\f$ where \f$\log f\f$ fails to be smooth at parameters \f$\theta\f$. |

## Parameter descriptor

The structure \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_param_info "distrib_param_info" describes analytical properties of a parameter:
- `name`: string identifier of the parameter (such as `"mu"`, `"sigma"`, `"phi"`).
- `lower`, `upper`: open natural domain \f$(lower, upper)\f$, with infinite limits represented by \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_infinity "distrib_infinity".
- `circular`: boolean indicating whether the parameter domain is circular (periodic).
- `regularity`: order of differentiability \f$r\f$ up to which the log-density \f$\log f(y; \theta)\f$ is continuous in this parameter. If the function is infinitely differentiable, this field holds \ref laplace::distrib::infinitely_smooth "infinitely_smooth"; otherwise, it holds a non-negative integer \f$r\f$, indicating that \f$\log f\f$ is \f$C^r\f$ with non-smooth points returned by `param_kinks()`.
- `moves_support`: boolean indicating whether varying this parameter moves the boundary points of the distribution support.
- `cdf_regularity`: order of differentiability up to which the cumulative functions \f$\log F(y; \theta)\f$ and \f$\log(1 - F(y; \theta))\f$ are continuous in this parameter.

### Interpretation of regularity and kinks

For smooth distributions such as \ref laplace::distrib::gaussian1_distrib "gaussian1_distrib", both parameters have `regularity = infinitely_smooth` and `cdf_regularity = infinitely_smooth`.

For distributions with non-smooth points, regularity fields state the exact degree of smoothness:
- In \ref laplace::distrib::laplace1_distrib "laplace1_distrib", the location parameter \f$\mu\f$ has `regularity = 0` and `cdf_regularity = 1`.
- The log-density \f$\log f(y; \mu, \sigma) = -\log(2\sigma) - |y - \mu|/\sigma\f$ is \f$C^0\f$ in \f$\mu\f$ at \f$\mu = y\f$; its first derivative in \f$\mu\f$ has a step discontinuity, and its pointwise second derivative is zero almost everywhere.
- Because the second derivative in \f$\mu\f$ carries a Dirac point mass at the kink \f$\mu = y\f$, the pointwise expectation \f$-\mathbb{E}[\frac{\partial^2}{\partial\mu^2}\log f]\f$ vanishes and does not equal the Fisher information. The expected Fisher information is defined by the variance of the score:
  \f[
  I_{\mu\mu} = \mathbb{E}\left[\left(\frac{\partial}{\partial\mu}\log f\right)^2\right] = \frac{1}{\sigma^2}
  \f]
- The cumulative distribution function \f$\log F(y)\f$ integrates the density, so it is one order smoother: it is \f$C^1\f$ at \f$\mu = y\f$, and its second derivative in \f$\mu\f$ jumps by \f$2/\sigma^2\f$ at that point.

Continuous distributions also specify `y_regularity`, the differentiability order of \f$\log f(y; \theta)\f$ with respect to the observation \f$y\f$:
- For smooth distributions such as \ref laplace::distrib::gaussian1_distrib "gaussian1_distrib" and \ref laplace::distrib::gamma1_distrib "gamma1_distrib", `y_regularity = infinitely_smooth`.
- In \ref laplace::distrib::laplace1_distrib "laplace1_distrib", `y_regularity = 0`, indicating that \f$\log f\f$ is \f$C^0\f$ in \f$y\f$ with a kink at \f$y = \mu\f$ reported by `y_kinks()`.

## Symmetric tensors

Derivatives of order \f$K\f$ with respect to \f$P\f$ parameters are symmetric tensors. In Laplace, these tensors are represented by \ref laplace::distrib::sym_tensor "sym_tensor<P, K>", storing only unique components.

### Storage and sizing

The number of unique components in a symmetric tensor of order \f$K\f$ over \f$P\f$ parameters is given by \ref laplace::distrib::sym_tensor_size "sym_tensor_size(P, K)":
\f[
\mathrm{size}(P, K) = \binom{P + K - 1}{K}
\f]
Components are packed in an array `double v[size]` indexed by sorted multi-indices \f$0 \le i_0 \le i_1 \le \dots \le i_{K-1} < P\f$ in lexicographic order.

The linear position of a sorted multi-index \f$(i_0, \dots, i_{K-1})\f$ is computed by \ref laplace::distrib::sym_tensor_position "sym_tensor_position<P, K>()":
\f[
\mathrm{pos}(i_0, \dots, i_{K-1}) = \sum_{j=0}^{K-1} \sum_{v=\mathrm{prev}}^{i_j - 1} \binom{P - v + K - j - 2}{K - j - 1}
\f]
where \f$\mathrm{prev} = 0\f$ for \f$j = 0\f$ and \f$\mathrm{prev} = i_{j-1}\f$ for \f$j > 0\f$.

### Worked example for two parameters and order 3

Consider \f$P = 2\f$ parameters (indices 0 and 1) and derivative order \f$K = 3\f$:
- Total components: \f$\binom{2 + 3 - 1}{3} = \binom{4}{3} = 4\f$.
- The sorted multi-indices in lexicographic order are:
  1. `(0, 0, 0)`: 3 derivatives in \f$\theta_0\f$, 0 in \f$\theta_1\f$. Stored at index `v[0]`.
  2. `(0, 0, 1)`: 2 derivatives in \f$\theta_0\f$, 1 in \f$\theta_1\f$. Stored at index `v[1]`.
  3. `(0, 1, 1)`: 1 derivative in \f$\theta_0\f$, 2 in \f$\theta_1\f$. Stored at index `v[2]`.
  4. `(1, 1, 1)`: 0 derivatives in \f$\theta_0\f$, 3 in \f$\theta_1\f$. Stored at index `v[3]`.

For any two-parameter distribution (\f$P = 2\f$) and derivative order \f$K\f$, the component with \f$b\f$ derivatives in the second parameter \f$\theta_1\f$ and \f$K - b\f$ derivatives in the first parameter \f$\theta_0\f$ is located at array index \f$b\f$ (`v[b]`).

### Packing of mixed derivatives in observation and parameters

For continuous distributions, `logpdf_dy_dk<J, K>(y)` computes the mixed partial derivatives:
\f[
\frac{\partial^{J+K}}{\partial y^J \partial\theta^K} \log f(y; \theta)
\f]
evaluating \f$J\f$ derivatives with respect to the observation argument \f$y\f$ and an order-\f$K\f$ symmetric derivative tensor with respect to the natural parameters \f$\theta\f$, for total order \f$J + K \le 6\f$. In mathematical terms, these evaluate derivatives in the argument of the density, allowing evaluation at an argument that is itself differentiated.

Each pair \f$(J, K)\f$ is a separate member template instantiation evaluated only when called, leaving the parameter-only derivatives `logpdf_d1()` through `logpdf_d6()` unchanged. For \f$J = 0, K = 0\f$, it evaluates the log-density value in `v[0]`, and for \f$J = 0, K \ge 1\f$, it matches `logpdf_dk<K>(y)`. Discrete distributions do not provide derivatives in \f$y\f$.

Packing follows the standard tensor ordering:
- The \f$J\f$ derivatives in \f$y\f$ are scalar and precede the parameter tensor.
- The derivatives in the \f$P\f$ parameters \f$\theta\f$ form a symmetric tensor of order \f$K\f$, returned as \ref laplace::distrib::sym_tensor "sym_tensor<P, K>".
- Its \f$\binom{P + K - 1}{K}\f$ components are packed over sorted multi-indices \f$0 \le i_0 \le \dots \le i_{K-1} < P\f$ in lexicographic order, identical to \ref laplace::distrib::sym_tensor_position "sym_tensor_position<P, K>()".
- For \f$K = 0\f$, the container is \ref laplace::distrib::sym_tensor "sym_tensor<P, 0>", which has size 1; the pure derivative \f$\frac{\partial^J}{\partial y^J} \log f(y; \theta)\f$ is stored at array index `v[0]`.
- For two parameters (\f$P = 2\f$) and any order \f$K \ge 0\f$, the component with \f$b\f$ derivatives in \f$\theta_1\f$ and \f$K - b\f$ in \f$\theta_0\f$ is stored at array index `v[b]`.

## Expected information tensor

The expected Fisher information matrix \f$I(\theta) = -\mathbb{E}\left[\frac{\partial^2}{\partial\theta^2}\log f\right]\f$ is positive definite. Because \f$I(\theta)\f$ is symmetric of size \f$P \times P\f$, it is stored as a \ref laplace::distrib::sym_tensor "sym_tensor<P, 2>" over parameter pairs \f$0 \le a \le b < P\f$. The number of unique pairs is \f$\binom{P + 1}{2}\f$.

Derivatives of the information matrix of order \f$K \in \{1, \dots, 4\}\f$ are represented by \ref laplace::distrib::expected_info_tensor "expected_info_tensor<P, K>":
- Holds an array `sym_tensor<P, K> pair[n_pairs]`.
- For each pair \f$a \le b\f$ in `sym_tensor<P, 2>` order, `pair[p]` holds the order-\f$K\f$ symmetric derivative tensor of \f$I_{ab}(\theta)\f$.
- Together with the 2 derivatives of \f$I\f$, this provides derivatives of log-likelihood expectations up to total order 6.

## Non-smooth points

The structure \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_kinks "distrib_kinks" characterizes points where a distribution or its derivatives fail to be infinitely smooth:
- `count`: number of non-smooth points (at most 2).
- `point[2]`: coordinates of the non-smooth points.
- `continuous_order`: highest derivative order that remains continuous across the kink.

A distribution provides two kink inspection functions:
- `param_kinks(j, y)`: returns kinks in the \f$j\f$-th parameter \f$\theta_j\f$ for a given observation \f$y\f$.
- `y_kinks()`: returns kinks in observation space \f$y\f$ for the distribution's current parameters.

## Measurement of tensor error

For scalar functions, error is quantified in units in the last place (ulp) against high-precision reference values; see \ref accuracy "How accuracy is measured".

For symmetric derivative tensors \f$v \in \mathbb{R}^M\f$ of order \f$K\f$, individual components frequently cross zero at different locations across the parameter space. At a zero of a single component, plain relative ulp error diverges even when the absolute error is on the order of machine epsilon.

To quantify tensor accuracy robustly, Laplace defines the error in ulp of the largest component of the order. Let \f$hi_i, lo_i\f$ be the double-double reference values for component \f$i \in \{0, \dots, M-1\}\f$, and let
\f[
S = \max_{0 \le i < M} |hi_i|
\f]
be the magnitude of the largest component of that order at the evaluation point. The error in ulp of the largest component is:
\f[
\text{error}_{\text{scaled}} = \max_{0 \le i < M} \frac{|(v_i - hi_i) - lo_i|}{\mathrm{ulp}(S)}
\f]
where \f$\mathrm{ulp}(S)\f$ is the spacing of floating-point numbers at scale \f$S\f$. This metric measures the accuracy of the tensor relative to its dominant term, preventing artificial error inflation at zeros of sub-dominant components.

## Kernel execution example

Distributions are designed to be instantiated and evaluated inside Kokkos kernels:

\code{.cpp}
#include <laplace/distrib/gaussian1.hpp>
#include <Kokkos_Core.hpp>

void evaluate_gaussian_kernel(Kokkos::View<const double*> y,
                              Kokkos::View<double*> log_lik,
                              Kokkos::View<double*> score_mu,
                              Kokkos::View<double*> info_mu,
                              double mu_val, double sigma_val) {
  const int n = y.extent(0);
  Kokkos::parallel_for("eval_gaussian", n, KOKKOS_LAMBDA(const int i) {
    // Construct distribution for observation
    const laplace::distrib::gaussian1_distrib dist{mu_val, sigma_val};

    // Evaluate log-density
    log_lik(i) = dist.logpdf(y(i));

    // Evaluate first derivative in parameters: v[0] is d/dmu, v[1] is d/dsigma
    const auto score = dist.logpdf_d1(y(i));
    score_mu(i) = score[0];

    // Evaluate expected information: pair[0] is (mu, mu)
    const auto info = dist.expected_info();
    info_mu(i) = info[0];
  });
}
\endcode

## Module types and functions

Types and utilities defined in `laplace::distrib`:

### Types

- \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_kind "distrib_kind" — Enumeration of distribution kinds (`continuous` or `discrete`).
- \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_param_info "distrib_param_info" — Descriptor of natural parameter properties.
- \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_kinks "distrib_kinks" — Description of non-smooth points and continuity orders.
- \ref laplace::distrib::sym_tensor "sym_tensor" — Fixed-size container for packed symmetric tensors.
- \ref laplace::distrib::expected_info_tensor "expected_info_tensor" — Container for derivatives of expected Fisher information.

### Constants

- \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_infinity "distrib_infinity" — IEEE floating-point infinity for distribution support and domain bounds.
- \ref laplace::distrib::infinitely_smooth "infinitely_smooth" — Regularity constant for infinitely differentiable parameters.

### Combinatorial functions

- \ref laplace::distrib::binomial_coef "binomial_coef()" — Computes the binomial coefficient \f$\binom{n}{k}\f$.
- \ref laplace::distrib::sym_tensor_size "sym_tensor_size()" — Number of unique components in a symmetric tensor of order \f$K\f$ over \f$P\f$ parameters.
- \ref laplace::distrib::sym_tensor_position "sym_tensor_position()" — Linear storage index for a sorted multi-index in a packed symmetric tensor.
*/
